As a web developer and Linux user, I spend a lot of time in a terminal. Whether it be in a WSL2 Ubuntu terminal on Windows or a Kitty terminal on Arch Linux, the default styling for the terminals need a lot of work.

Here is how I customize my terminals!

# Switch to zsh

Usually, Linux terminals use the default `bash` shell. If you are using the `bash` shell, I highly recommend switching to `zsh`, as it provides a number of nice improvements, such as:

- Context-aware autocompletion
- Spelling corrections
- Shared command history (command ran in Terminal 1 is available to Terminal 2 via the up-arrow).

```echo
You can check what shell you are running currently, by using this command:echo $0
```
